  "modules": [
    {
      "id": "il06-hackers-arise",
      "code": "IL-06",
      "title": "The Hackers-Arise Spine",
      "summary": "Welcome to IL-06 — Cybersecurity & Secure Engineering. I’m your instructor, and we begin the way every serious security operator begins: by learning how systems break. This is Module 1, the Hackers-Arise spine — Linux for hackers, TCP/IP for attackers, OSINT tradecraft, password cracking, Wi-Fi hacking, web application exploitation, forensics, and SCADA/ICS plus satellite systems — cross-mapped to labs and the NICE Framework, with safety-critical ethics in every lesson. Everything offensive you practice here is for legal targets only, with documented authorization. Know the attack to earn the defense — that contract runs through the whole course.",
      "objectives": [
        "Use Linux as an operations platform: bash, text processing at speed.",
        "Map TCP/IP from the attacker's view: recon, scanning, exploitation workflow.",
        "Practice OSINT tradecraft with documentation discipline.",
        "Crack passwords to learn credential hygiene; hack Wi-Fi to learn defense."
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **bash for operations** — the shell as an operations platform: looping over targets, parsing command output at speed.\n- **text processing pipeline** — grep, sed, awk, cut, sort, and uniq chained over scan output; the attacker’s spreadsheet.\n- **SUID** — the set-user-ID permission bit: a binary that runs as its owner; the classic Linux privilege-escalation surface.\n- **persistence mechanism** — cron jobs and services, the footholds that survive reboots; you learn them to recognize them.\n- **TCP/IP for attackers** — reading what each header field leaks: TTL and window sizes hint the OS, sequence numbers hint the stack.\n- **host discovery** — finding which hosts are alive before you scan a single port; recon’s first question.\n- **connect scan** — a full TCP handshake per port; loud, reliable, and logged everywhere.\n- **SYN scan** — the half-open scan; quieter than a connect scan, and the attacker’s default.\n- **UDP scan** — slow and unreliable, but UDP services hide some of the best vulnerabilities.\n- **vulnerability scanning** — service detection plus version fingerprinting: identify the software, pin the exact version, match the known weakness.\n- **exploitation workflow** — recon → scan → exploit → post-exploitation; the grammar every attack in this course follows.\n- **passive reconnaissance** — DNS, WHOIS, certificate transparency, breach data, social media; zero contact with the target.\n- **active reconnaissance** — anything that touches the target; it announces you and requires authorization.\n- **OSINT tradecraft** — sock puppets and compartmentation; operational security for the investigator.\n- **documentation discipline** — every finding sourced and timestamped; the lab report starts at recon, not at the shell.\n- **hash identification** — recognizing the hash format first (hash-identifier thinking) so the attack matches the algorithm.\n- **rule-based cracking** — a wordlist plus mangling rules — leetspeak substitutions, ?d?d?d?d appends — multiplies a list roughly 100x; attackers think in rules, not bigger lists.\n- **keyspace** — the total number of candidate passwords; time-to-crack is keyspace divided by hashrate.\n- **credential hygiene** — the defender’s lesson from cracking: length, uniqueness, and MFA beat every clever password.\n- **802.11 fundamentals** — how Wi-Fi frames, beacons, and authentication actually work; the prerequisite to attacking or defending wireless.\n- **4-way handshake** — the WPA2 key exchange; capture it and the crack happens offline, at your speed.\n- **deauthentication attack** — forcing a client to reconnect so you can capture its handshake; on your own lab network only.\n- **WPA3** — the current Wi-Fi standard; resists the offline dictionary attacks that fell WPA2.\n- **PMF** — Protected Management Frames; the defense that defeats deauthentication attacks.\n- **rogue-AP detection** — finding evil-twin access points impersonating your network.\n- **Burp methodology** — proxy to observe, repeater to manipulate by hand, intruder to automate, decoder to transform; methodology before automation.\n- **injection flaw** — SQL injection, command injection, cross-site scripting; untrusted input reaching an interpreter is the vulnerability.\n- **attack surface** — in web exploitation, every input field, header, and parameter is the attack surface.\n- **authentication attack** — brute force, session-handling abuse, MFA bypass shapes; identity is the perimeter now.\n- **disk forensics** — file carving, deleted-file recovery, registry and USN analysis; the artifacts your attack leaves behind.\n- **memory forensics** — process lists, injected code, network connections; memory is where malware lives.\n- **super-timeline** — filesystem, registry, and log events merged into one chronological view of the intrusion.\n- **anti-forensics** — timestomping, log wiping; you learn it so you can detect it.\n- **Modbus** — the OT protocol with no authentication by design; you don’t patch it, you segment around it.\n- **DNP3** — the utility-sector control protocol; the same unauthenticated heritage as Modbus.\n- **air-gapped lab** — the only acceptable OT practice environment: no route to production, ever.\n- **safety-critical ethics** — OT attacks can kill people; that fact outranks every technique in this module.\n- **NICE Framework** — the workforce taxonomy this spine maps to: OG (Oversight/Governance), DD (Design/Development), IO (Implementation/Operation), PD (Protection/Defense), IN (Investigation)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "Before you touch an offensive tool, you build the lab and the habits. This is the exact sequence I’d run with a new operator, and it’s your Milestone 1.1. Everything here runs against systems you own.\n1. **Build the lab first.** Two virtual machines on an isolated host-only network: an attacker Linux box and a target you own. No lab, no techniques — that’s the rule.\n2. **Bash warm-up: pipeline a scan.** Run a scan of your lab target, then chain grep, cut, sort, and uniq to extract just the open ports and service banners. Text processing at speed is the skill; the scan is the excuse.\n3. **Passive recon drill.** Against a domain you own: pull certificate-transparency names, DNS records, and WHOIS. Zero packets to the target’s ports — passive before active is ethics AND tradecraft.\n4. **Scan with discipline.** One host deeply before ten hosts shallowly. Compare a connect scan against a SYN scan of the same lab host and note what each reveals — and what each would log.\n5. **Identify, then crack, a lab hash.** Practice hash-identifier thinking on a hash you generated yourself, then attack it with a wordlist plus rules. Estimate the time first: keyspace divided by hashrate.\n6. **Wi-Fi on your own access point.** Capture your own 4-way handshake, crack it offline, then enable WPA3 with PMF and confirm the attack no longer works. Every attack module ends in the defense — that’s the spine’s contract.\n7. **Burp walkthrough.** Proxy a lab web application: observe in the proxy, manipulate one request by hand in the repeater, then automate one input with the intruder. Methodology before automation.\n8. **Timeline your own attack.** Build a super-timeline covering steps 2 through 7 and note every artifact you left. If you can’t find your own footprints, you can’t find an attacker’s."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "**Capstone: the spine field manual.** Produce your personal operator’s field manual — one page per spine domain (Linux, networks, OSINT, passwords, Wi-Fi, web, forensics, OT/satellite). Each page holds: the five commands or concepts you’d reach for first, one lab you actually ran with its key output, and the defensive countermeasure that defeats the attack. Append your command log from the Hands-on procedure and a one-page NICE self-map (OG/DD/IO/PD/IN) showing where your skills sit today. The manual is graded on accuracy and on the defense column — an attack page without its countermeasure is incomplete."
        },
        {
          "title": "Noah check-in",
          "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- In one sentence, what is the deliverable I’m producing by the end of this module?\n- Walk Noah through your recon order on a new lab target — and name the exact point where passive becomes active, and why that line matters.\n- *Hands-on:* “Show Noah your text-processing pipeline over real scan output: open ports and banners only, no noise.”\n- *Ethics — Noah’s rule:* name the authorization for every technique you practiced this module. No authorization, no technique. Say it out loud."
        }
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "front": "Passive vs active recon?",
          "back": "Passive: no target contact (DNS, OSINT). Active: touches the target (scanning) — announces you, needs authorization."
        },
        {
          "front": "Rule-based cracking advantage?",
          "back": "Rules (append digits, leetspeak) multiply a wordlist ~100x — attackers think in rules, not bigger lists."
        },
        {
          "front": "WPA handshake attack chain?",
          "back": "Deauth client → capture 4-way handshake → crack offline. Defense: WPA3, PMF."
        },
        {
          "front": "Burp tool order?",
          "back": "Proxy (observe) → Repeater (manual) → Intruder (automate) → Decoder. Methodology before automation."
        },
        {
          "front": "Modbus security model?",
          "back": "None — unauthenticated by design. OT security is network segmentation + monitoring."
        },
        {
          "front": "NICE categories (5)?",
          "back": "OG Oversight/Governance, DD Design/Development, IO Implementation/Operation, PD Protection/Defense, IN Investigation."
        },
        {
          "front": "OSINT documentation discipline?",
          "back": "Source, date, method, and confidence on every finding — recon you can't cite is recon you can't defend in a report."
        },
        {
          "front": "WPA3-SAE fixes what?",
          "back": "Offline dictionary attacks on the WPA2 handshake — Dragonfly makes each password guess require a live exchange with the AP."
        }
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "First recon step on a new engagement?",
          "choices": [
            "port scan everything",
            "passive: DNS, certs, OSINT — no target contact",
            "exploit immediately",
            "social engineer"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Passive reconnaissance comes first: silent, authorized-safe, and it often reveals the attack path. The Hands-on procedure runs passive before active."
        },
        {
          "q": "Hash-identifier says MD5crypt. Best approach?",
          "choices": [
            "bigger wordlist only",
            "wordlist + rules (leetspeak, appends) matched to the target's patterns",
            "give up",
            "rainbow tables only"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Rule-based cracking multiplies the wordlist; target-aware rules beat raw size. The Concepts section’s hash-identification entry."
        },
        {
          "q": "Deauth attack captured a handshake. Next (legal lab)?",
          "choices": [
            "crack offline with wordlist+rules",
            "attack neighbors",
            "publish it",
            "nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "Crack offline against your own lab handshake — the authorized workflow from the Hands-on procedure. Then enable WPA3 and PMF, per the Project brief’s defense column."
        },
        {
          "q": "SQLi found in login form. Professional next step?",
          "choices": [
            "dump the whole DB",
            "document, assess impact minimally, report — stay in scope",
            "escalate to RCE immediately",
            "tell the internet"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Prove impact minimally, document, report — scope is the law. The Concepts section’s documentation discipline."
        },
        {
          "q": "Modbus traffic on an OT network. Defensive priority?",
          "choices": [
            "patch Modbus",
            "segment the OT network + monitor — protocol can't be secured",
            "ignore",
            "encrypt Modbus"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Modbus is unauthenticated by design — defense is segmentation and monitoring, never patching the protocol. The Concepts section, with the air-gapped-lab rule."
        },
        {
          "q": "NICE PD category covers?",
          "choices": [
            "HR",
            "Protection and Defense — the blue-team roles",
            "finance",
            "legal only"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "PD is Protection and Defense — the blue-team workforce roles in the NICE Framework. The Concepts section."
        },
        {
          "q": "A lab binary has the SUID bit set and is owned by root. Why does an attacker care?",
          "choices": [
            "it runs faster",
            "it executes with the owner’s privileges — root — so an exploitable SUID binary is a privilege-escalation path",
            "it is hidden from ls",
            "it cannot be deleted"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "SUID runs the binary as its owner; root-owned plus exploitable equals privesc. Covered in the Concepts section."
        },
        {
          "q": "Why is the SYN scan the attacker’s default rather than the connect scan?",
          "choices": [
            "it is faster on UDP",
            "the half-open handshake is quieter and less likely to be logged than a full connect scan",
            "it works without a network",
            "it finds more ports"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "SYN scans never complete the handshake, so they’re stealthier than full connects. Covered in the Concepts section."
        },
        {
          "q": "Time-to-crack a password is estimated as…",
          "choices": [
            "wordlist size times luck",
            "keyspace divided by hashrate",
            "hash length minus salt",
            "CPU cores plus RAM"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Total candidates divided by guesses per second gives the time estimate — the rig/time math from the Concepts section."
        },
        {
          "q": "You enable WPA3 with PMF on your lab access point. What did you just defeat?",
          "choices": [
            "nothing — wireless is insecure",
            "the offline handshake-cracking chain and deauthentication attacks",
            "all malware",
            "Bluetooth attacks"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "WPA3 resists offline dictionary attacks; PMF defeats deauth. Every attack ends in the defense — the Project brief’s rule."
        }
      ],
      "weight": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "il06-offensive",
      "code": "IL-06",
      "title": "Offensive Operations: HTB, THM, OffSec",
      "summary": "Welcome back — I’m your instructor, and this is Module 2: offensive operations. In Module 1 you learned how systems break; now you learn to break them methodically, the way a professional penetration tester does: lab operations methodology with enumeration discipline, the PEN-200/OSCP map including buffer overflows and the 24-hour exam logistics, advanced web exploitation chains, Active Directory attack chains with BloodHound thinking, the T0–T3 CTF architecture, and professional reporting — because the report is the product. Legal targets only, documented authorization, stay in scope. The exploit is the receipt; enumeration is the purchase.",
      "objectives": [
        "Run the machine methodology: enumerate deeply, exploit methodically, document always.",
        "Execute AD attack chains: Kerberoasting, BloodHound pathfinding, lateral movement.",
        "Chain web vulnerabilities: SQLi→RCE, SSRF→cloud metadata, deserialization.",
        "Write findings that get fixed: severity, evidence, impact, remediation."
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **machine methodology** — recon → enumerate → exploit → privilege-escalate → loot → report; the loop you run on every lab box.\n- **enumeration discipline** — every port, every service, every version; the vulnerability hides in the boring service you skipped.\n- **Linux privesc patterns** — SUID binaries, cron jobs, writable PATH and service files, kernel version, sudo rights; the checklist you run on every shell.\n- **Windows privesc patterns** — unquoted service paths, weak service permissions, AlwaysInstallElevated, token privileges; the Windows twin of the Linux checklist.\n- **‘try harder’, correctly understood** — enumerate more, not guess more; the PEN-200 methodology in three words.\n- **buffer overflow** — the rite of passage: fuzz to crash, find the offset, control EIP, deliver shellcode; it teaches the stack disguised as an exploit.\n- **EIP control** — overwriting the instruction pointer; the moment a crash becomes an exploit.\n- **DEP/ASLR** — Data Execution Prevention and Address Space Layout Randomization; the mitigations that make classic overflows a fundamentals lesson.\n- **SQLi→RCE** — turning SQL injection into remote code execution: xp_cmdshell, writing web shells; the web chain that ends in a shell.\n- **SSRF** — server-side request forgery: making the server fetch a URL you chose; the pivot from web bug to infrastructure access.\n- **cloud metadata service** — 169.254.169.254: instance credentials over HTTP; the SSRF crown jewel and the keys to the kingdom.\n- **IMDSv2 defense** — hop limits and token requirements that blunt SSRF-to-metadata attacks; know the defense with the attack.\n- **deserialization** — untrusted object graphs decoded into live objects; gadget chains turn it into remote code execution.\n- **race condition (TOCTOU)** — time-of-check to time-of-use: the timing attack that wins when two operations interleave wrong.\n- **Kerberoasting** — requesting crackable (RC4) service tickets as any domain user, then cracking them offline; the service account’s password, no privileges required.\n- **AS-REP roasting** — accounts with Kerberos pre-authentication disabled let you request the AS-REP directly and crack it offline.\n- **BloodHound thinking** — attack the graph, not the host: find the shortest path to Domain Admin through misconfigurations first, exploit second.\n- **lateral movement** — PSExec, WMI, WinRM, pass-the-hash; moving through the network with captured credentials.\n- **pass-the-hash** — authenticating with the hash itself, no cracking required; why hashes are password-equivalent.\n- **Golden Ticket** — a forged Kerberos ticket-granting ticket; domain dominance that betrays itself in ticket metadata.\n- **DCSync** — replicating directory secrets like a domain controller; detected in event 4662 replication anomalies.\n- **event 4769** — the Kerberos service-ticket request log; anomalies here are the Kerberoasting detection.\n- **T0–T3 CTF architecture** — T0 guided warmups, T1 single-technique, T2 chained scenarios, T3 research-grade original problems.\n- **finding write-up** — title, severity, CVSS, evidence, business impact, remediation, retest; the anatomy of a finding that gets fixed.\n- **risk rating** — likelihood times impact, stated honestly; inflating severity burns credibility at retest.\n- **executive summary** — the one page the board actually reads; findings are for engineers, summaries are for decisions.\n- **retest verification** — proving the fix worked; the step where client trust compounds."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "This module is a discipline module: the methodology matters more than any single exploit. Run this exact loop on every lab machine, and document from minute one — screenshots and commands, because the report starts at recon.\n1. **Enumerate like it’s 70% of the work — because it is.** Scan every port, identify every service, pin every version. The unexamined UDP service is where the vulnerability lives; check it before you touch the web app.\n2. **Run the privesc checklists.** On a Linux shell: SUID binaries, cron jobs, writable paths, kernel version, sudo rights. On a Windows shell: unquoted paths, service permissions, AlwaysInstallElevated, tokens. Checklists beat inspiration.\n3. **Walk the buffer-overflow rite of passage.** In the lab: fuzz to crash, find the offset, control EIP, build the exploit. You’re here to learn the stack — registers, memory layout, DEP/ASLR — disguised as an exploit.\n4. **Chain a web vulnerability.** Take SQL injection to code execution in the lab (xp_cmdshell or a written shell), then pivot an SSRF to the metadata service at 169.254.169.254 — and then configure the IMDSv2 defense and confirm the chain breaks.\n5. **Map Active Directory as a graph.** Ingest lab AD data into BloodHound-style thinking: find the shortest path to Domain Admin before you exploit anything. Then walk it: Kerberoasting or AS-REP roasting for the first credential, lateral movement for the next hop.\n6. **Pair every attack with its detection.** Kerberoasting → event 4769 anomalies. DCSync → 4662. Golden Ticket → ticket metadata. Red teaches blue — log the detection alongside the exploit in your notes.\n7. **Climb one CTF tier.** Solve at your current tier (T0 guided through T3 research) and write the solve up as a finding: title, evidence, impact, remediation — even for a game box. The report is the product.\n8. **Exam logistics rehearsal.** If OSCP is on your roadmap: plan a 24-hour practice block — sleep strategy, meal breaks, and starting the report early. The report has its own 48-hour clock; logistics are part of the methodology."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "**Capstone: the engagement report.** Run a full authorized penetration test against your lab network — minimum one Linux host, one Windows host, one web application, and an Active Directory lab — and deliver the professional report: executive summary (the page the board reads), methodology, findings with severity, CVSS, evidence, business impact, and remediation for each, plus a retest plan. Grade yourself on the honesty rules from the Concepts section: risk ratings at likelihood-times-impact with no inflation, every finding carrying its fix, and scope discipline documented. A finding without remediation is a complaint; access without a report is a story."
        },
        {
          "title": "Noah check-in",
          "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- In one sentence, what is the deliverable I’m producing by the end of this module?\n- *Enumeration drill:* “Noah hands you a scan with one unexamined UDP service. Talk Noah through your next three commands and why.”\n- Draw the AD attack chain from the Hands-on procedure as a graph: where does BloodHound thinking save you the most time, and which hop has the clearest detection?\n- *Honesty check:* you found a critical but the evidence is thin. Do you inflate, downgrade, or qualify — and what does the retest do to each choice?"
        }
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "front": "Enumeration share of the work?",
          "back": "~70%. The exploit is the receipt; enumeration is the purchase."
        },
        {
          "front": "Linux privesc checklist?",
          "back": "SUID binaries, cron jobs, writable PATH/service files, kernel version, sudo rights."
        },
        {
          "front": "Kerberoasting in one line?",
          "back": "Request crackable service tickets (RC4) as any user → crack offline → the service account's password."
        },
        {
          "front": "BloodHound's insight?",
          "back": "Attack the graph: shortest path to Domain Admin via misconfigurations, not host-by-host."
        },
        {
          "front": "SSRF cloud target?",
          "back": "169.254.169.254 — instance metadata; IMDSv2 hop limits defend it."
        },
        {
          "front": "Report anatomy?",
          "back": "Title, severity/CVSS, evidence, business impact, remediation, retest. Access without a report is a story."
        },
        {
          "front": "Golden Ticket vs Silver Ticket?",
          "back": "Golden: forged TGT from the krbtgt hash — any service, any user, domain-wide. Silver: forged TGS for one service — quieter, narrower."
        },
        {
          "front": "AS-REP roasting needs?",
          "back": "An account with Kerberos pre-authentication disabled — request its AS-REP and crack the encrypted timestamp offline. No credentials required."
        }
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
