    {
      "id": "il06-defensive",
      "code": "IL-06",
      "title": "Defensive Operations: SOC to Incident Response",
      "summary": "Welcome back — I’m your instructor, and this is Module 3: defensive operations. Red team taught you how the break-in works; now you learn the other half of the skill: SOC operations across the three tiers, forensics and incident response at SANS depth, detection engineering with Sigma, and the Security+ bridge with its readiness gate. Red and blue are two halves of one skill — every attack you learned in Module 2 has a detection, and this module teaches you to build it. Contain before you eradicate, memory before disk, and blameless always.",
      "objectives": [
        "Triage SOC alerts: L1 speed, L2 depth, L3 hunting.",
        "Run the IR lifecycle: detect → contain → eradicate → recover → lessons-learned.",
        "Write and tune Sigma detections; manage false positives.",
        "Perform timeline analysis with chain-of-custody discipline."
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **L1 triage** — alert to quick classification: true or false positive, severity, escalate or close; speed with accuracy, measured by time-to-triage.\n- **L2 investigation** — pulling the logs, building the timeline, correlating across EDR, firewall, proxy, and identity; depth after the L1 handoff.\n- **L3 threat hunting** — hypothesis-driven searches (‘what would this adversary do here?’); hunts that find things become detections.\n- **detection engineering** — the discipline of turning hunts into deployed, tuned detections; the bridge between red knowledge and blue operations.\n- **Sigma** — the open detection format: title, logsource, detection (selection plus filter plus condition), level; write once, convert to Splunk, Elastic, or Sentinel.\n- **logsource** — the Sigma field naming what the rule watches; a rule is only as good as its log source’s coverage.\n- **false positive tuning** — baseline, threshold, allowlist; false positives are the tax on detection, budgeted and tuned — never silently tolerated.\n- **blind spot** — what a log source cannot tell you; blind spots drive the logging roadmap, because you can’t detect what you don’t log.\n- **disk forensics** — file carving, deleted-file recovery, registry and USN analysis; the persistent record of the intrusion.\n- **memory forensics** — process lists, injected code, network connections; memory is where malware lives — and it evaporates.\n- **network forensics** — PCAP timelines and C2 beacons: regular intervals plus consistent sizes are the beacon pattern.\n- **order of volatility** — memory before disk, always; collect the ephemeral first in live response.\n- **IR lifecycle** — preparation → detection and analysis → containment → eradication → recovery → lessons learned; the exam and the job agree on this order.\n- **short-term containment** — isolate the host now; stop the bleeding before you diagnose.\n- **long-term containment** — rebuild and harden; the fix that survives the attacker’s return.\n- **chain of custody** — hash everything (SHA-256) and document every hand; evidence that survives challenge.\n- **super-timeline** — log2timeline-style merged timelines; the story of the intrusion in one view.\n- **threat-informed defense** — mapping detections to MITRE ATT&CK; coverage gaps become the hunting backlog, and vague ‘better detection’ becomes a shopping list.\n- **impossible travel** — same identity, two distant locations, minutes apart; paired with a fresh MFA enrollment it’s the account-takeover pattern.\n- **JA3** — the TLS client fingerprint; odd JA3 plus beaconing intervals confirms C2 in PCAP.\n- **blameless postmortem** — fix the system, not the person; blame hides the next incident.\n- **lessons learned** — the IR report that prevents recurrence; an incident response without it is just firefighting.\n- **Security+ bridge** — the SY0-701 defensive vocabulary and readiness gate in this module; full preparation lives in IL-09."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "Blue-team skill is built in the lab exactly like red-team skill: repetition against realistic data. You’ll work the full SOC motion — triage, investigate, hunt, detect — and finish by running an incident end to end.\n1. **Triage drill: the 5-minute queue.** Take a set of lab alerts and classify each: true/false positive, severity, escalate or close. Time yourself — time-to-triage is the L1 metric. The impossible-travel plus new-MFA-enrollment alert escalates immediately; say why out loud.\n2. **Investigate one escalation.** Pull the logs for your escalated alert and correlate across at least three sources (endpoint, identity, network). Build the timeline before you conclude — correlation beats collection.\n3. **Hunt from a hypothesis.** Write one hypothesis (‘if the attacker used encoded PowerShell here, we’d see it in process-creation logs’), then search for it. Document the hypothesis even if the hunt finds nothing — failed hunts still teach the environment.\n4. **Turn the hunt into a Sigma rule.** Write the detection: title, logsource, selection plus filter plus condition, level. Then write the tuning note from the Concepts section: what will false-positive, and how you’d filter it.\n5. **Map it to ATT&CK.** Tag your rule to its technique and check your coverage: the gap you find becomes your next hunt. Threat-informed defense turns ‘we need better detection’ into a backlog.\n6. **Run the IR lifecycle tabletop.** Take the phishing-wave scenario: preparation, detection, short-term containment (isolate, revoke sessions), eradication, recovery — then write the lessons-learned. Contain before eradicating while scope is unknown.\n7. **Practice the order of volatility.** In a live-response lab: memory first (process lists, connections), disk second. Narrate what evaporates if you reverse the order.\n8. **Close the loop blamelessly.** Write the postmortem for your tabletop incident with zero blame on any analyst — every action item targets the system. That’s the deliverable culture."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "**Capstone: the SOC shift simulation.** You are the entire SOC for a shift. Ingest a prepared alert bundle (benign noise, two true positives, one account-takeover pattern, one C2 beacon): triage the queue inside a timebox, escalate with written rationale, investigate the top escalation across three log sources, run one hypothesis-driven hunt, author the Sigma rule your hunt justifies with its tuning note, and close with a blameless lessons-learned memo. Deliverables: triage log with times, investigation timeline, the Sigma rule, the ATT&CK coverage note, and the memo. Graded on the Concepts-section disciplines: containment before eradication, memory before disk, tuned — not tolerated — false positives."
        },
        {
          "title": "Noah check-in",
          "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- In one sentence, what is the deliverable I’m producing by the end of this module?\n- *Triage drill:* “Noah shows you an alert: impossible travel plus a new MFA enrollment. You have thirty seconds — escalate or close, and state the pattern.”\n- Your Sigma rule fires 500 times a day, all benign. Walk Noah through your tuning plan from the Hands-on procedure — and explain why silently disabling it is the wrong move.\n- *Honesty check:* the lessons-learned draft blames the L1 analyst. Rewrite the finding the blameless way, out loud."
        }
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "front": "SOC tiers?",
          "back": "L1 triage (speed), L2 investigation/correlation (depth), L3 hunting (hypotheses)."
        },
        {
          "front": "Contain vs eradicate order?",
          "back": "Contain first when scope is unknown — eradication lets attackers re-seed."
        },
        {
          "front": "Live response: memory or disk first?",
          "back": "Memory — it evaporates. Disk persists."
        },
        {
          "front": "Sigma rule anatomy?",
          "back": "Title, logsource, detection (selection/filter/condition), level. One rule, many SIEMs."
        },
        {
          "front": "Chain of custody?",
          "back": "Hash (SHA-256) + document every hand — evidence that survives challenge."
        },
        {
          "front": "IR's real deliverable?",
          "back": "Lessons learned — the report that prevents recurrence."
        },
        {
          "front": "Diamond Model's four vertices?",
          "back": "Adversary, Capability, Infrastructure, Victim — every intrusion event needs all four; pivot on the known ones to reveal the rest."
        },
        {
          "front": "ATT&CK tactic vs technique?",
          "back": "Tactic: the attacker's why — the goal (e.g., Persistence). Technique: the how — the method, with sub-techniques for variants."
        }
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
