    {
      "id": "il06-fde-interface",
      "code": "IL-06",
      "title": "The Forward-Deployed Interface",
      "summary": "Welcome back — I’m your instructor, and this is Module 4: the forward-deployed interface. This is where security engineering meets the customer: the forward-deployed engineer embedded in their network, under their compliance, on their timeline — diagnosing fast in unfamiliar estates, building to their constraints, and transferring everything so they can operate it after you leave. You’ll also practice AI-aided security work with verification discipline, threat modeling with STRIDE and DREAD as working tools, and shipping secure defaults through reviews that unblock instead of blocking. The deliverable is never just access — it’s the hardened system, the handoff, and the customer left stronger.",
      "objectives": [
        "Operate as a forward-deployed engineer: diagnose, build, and transfer under customer constraints.",
        "Apply AI as a SOC/hunting amplifier — with verification discipline.",
        "Threat-model systems: STRIDE/DREAD as working tools, not compliance theater.",
        "Ship secure defaults and run security reviews that unblock instead of blocking."
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **forward-deployed engineer** — the operator embedded with the customer: their network, their compliance, their timeline; diagnose fast, build to constraints, transfer everything.\n- **transfer** — the FDE’s real deliverable: documentation and handoff so the customer operates the system after you leave.\n- **unfamiliar estate** — a customer environment you’ve never seen; rapid diagnosis there is the FDE’s core skill.\n- **AI triage summarization** — models drafting incident summaries from alert context; the analyst verifies before acting.\n- **KQL from English** — AI-generated hunt queries; useful drafts that get a human check before they touch production data.\n- **verification discipline** — the model drafts, the analyst decides; every AI output gets a human check before action.\n- **AI-assisted hunting** — hypothesis generation from threat intel and anomaly narration; the amplifier, not the analyst.\n- **practitioner-led learning** — war stories over lectures; operators carry the tacit knowledge slides can’t.\n- **threat modeling** — enumerating what you’re defending, from whom, and where it breaks — early, at design time.\n- **STRIDE** — Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege; applied per component.\n- **DREAD** — Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, Discoverability; the ranking scale for what STRIDE finds.\n- **mitigations with owners** — the only acceptable threat-model output: each finding paired with a fix and a name.\n- **model rot** — threat models decay on change like milk, not wine; a new data flow triggers a new session.\n- **secure defaults** — deny-by-default, least privilege out of the box, APIs that are hard to misuse; defaults decide outcomes.\n- **deny-by-default** — nothing is allowed until explicitly permitted; the default posture that defeats most breaches.\n- **least privilege** — every component gets the minimum access it needs; the principle behind the default.\n- **security review as a service** — findings delivered with fixes and SLAs by severity; the review that unblocks instead of blocking.\n- **review SLA** — critical findings in hours, highs in days; predictability is what turns security from gate to partner.\n- **shipping at the boundary** — the customer’s auditors, change windows, and rollback needs; security that ships beats perfect security that doesn’t.\n- **auditor-ready documentation** — writing like an auditor reads it, because at the boundary one will."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "Security engineering at the boundary is a practice discipline: model, decide, ship, hand off. Run this sequence against a sample system — a small web service with a database and an admin panel is plenty.\n1. **Scope the model.** List what you’re defending, from whom, and the trust boundaries. One whiteboard, one hour — a whiteboard hour now saves a sprint of rework later.\n2. **STRIDE per component.** Walk each component through Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, DoS, and Elevation. Write each finding as a concrete abuse case, not a vague worry.\n3. **Rank with DREAD.** Score Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, and Discoverability for each finding. The ranking is what turns a list into a plan.\n4. **Assign mitigations with owners.** Every finding gets a fix and a name. A model without mitigations is a diagram — say that out loud before you close the session.\n5. **Harden the defaults.** Apply deny-by-default and least privilege to the sample service: remove the permissive default config, scope the service account, choose the safe API. Defaults decide outcomes — most breaches exploit the default, not the exotic.\n6. **Run the review as a service.** Write up your findings the way a review should land: each with its fix, each with an SLA (critical in hours, highs in days). Predictability builds trust.\n7. **AI-amplify one step.** Have an AI tool draft the incident summary or a KQL hunt for your sample system — then verify every line before you’d act on it. The model drafts, you decide.\n8. **Write the handoff.** Produce the transfer document: architecture, credentials handling, runbooks, rollback plan. The customer operates it after you leave — that’s the deliverable."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "**Capstone: the boundary engagement.** Take a sample customer scenario — regulated, change-window constrained, with an auditor in the loop — and deliver the full forward-deployed package: a STRIDE/DREAD threat model with mitigations and owners, the hardened-defaults configuration, a security review written as a service (findings with fixes and SLAs), and the transfer document with runbooks and rollback plan. Then write the one-page retrospective: where did the model rot risk live, and what triggers a re-model? Graded on the Concepts-section contract: every finding has an owner, every default is deny-first, and the handoff would survive the auditor."
        },
        {
          "title": "Noah check-in",
          "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- In one sentence, what is the deliverable I’m producing by the end of this module?\n- *Scenario:* “Noah is the customer: air-gapped, regulated, and your timeline just halved. Walk Noah through what you ship, what you cut, and what the transfer looks like.”\n- An AI draft says to isolate the domain controller. Talk Noah through your verification steps before you’d click — what’s blast-radius-critical here?\n- *Honesty check:* your threat model found nothing above medium. Do you ship it as-is, or does ‘nothing found’ deserve a second session? Defend your call."
        }
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "front": "FDE's real deliverable?",
          "back": "Transfer — the customer operates it after you leave. Docs + handoff."
        },
        {
          "front": "AI in SOC: the rule?",
          "back": "Model drafts, analyst decides — verify every AI output before action."
        },
        {
          "front": "STRIDE?",
          "back": "Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, DoS, Elevation — per component."
        },
        {
          "front": "DREAD?",
          "back": "Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, Discoverability — ranking."
        },
        {
          "front": "Threat model output?",
          "back": "Mitigations with owners — a model without mitigations is a diagram."
        },
        {
          "front": "Secure defaults examples?",
          "back": "Deny-by-default, least privilege OOTB, safe APIs hard to misuse."
        },
        {
          "front": "Threat model inputs?",
          "back": "Architecture diagram, data flows, trust boundaries, attacker personas — STRIDE each element, DREAD the findings, ship mitigations."
        },
        {
          "front": "A security review that unblocks?",
          "back": "Threat-model early, propose the secure default, and own the fix PR — reviews that only say no get routed around."
        }
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "Customer is air-gapped and regulated. FDE approach?",
          "choices": [
            "cloud-only solution",
            "build to their constraints: offline, auditable, transferable",
            "insist on your stack",
            "leave"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Their constraints rule: build offline, auditable, and transferable — the FDE role in the Concepts section."
        },
        {
          "q": "AI drafts an incident summary with a wrong timestamp. Lesson?",
          "choices": [
            "AI is useless",
            "verify before action — the analyst decides",
            "trust it",
            "skip summaries"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "The model drafts, the analyst decides — verify every AI output before action. The Concepts section’s verification discipline."
        },
        {
          "q": "New payment flow added. Threat model?",
          "choices": [
            "skip — small change",
            "new data flow → new model session",
            "annual only",
            "ask legal"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "New data flow, new model session — models rot like milk, not wine. The Hands-on procedure’s scoping step."
        },
        {
          "q": "STRIDE 'R' example?",
          "choices": [
            "DDoS",
            "attacker denies sending the transaction — need non-repudiation (signatures, logs)",
            "sniffing",
            "privesc"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "Repudiation — counter disputed actions with signed logs and audit trails. The Concepts section’s STRIDE entry."
        },
        {
          "q": "Security review blocks releases for weeks. Fix?",
          "choices": [
