    {
      "id": "il06-azure-soc",
      "code": "IL-06",
      "title": "Security Operations on Microsoft Sentinel: SC-200",
      "summary": "Welcome to the final module — I’m your instructor, and this is where everything converges: security operations on Microsoft Sentinel at SC-200 depth. You’ll manage the security operations environment (Defender XDR, Sentinel workspaces, data connectors, analytics rules, automation playbooks, MITRE ATT&CK mapping), respond to incidents across Defender, M365, and Purview with Graph enrichment, and hunt in KQL — hypotheses first, queries second, notebooks for repeatability — capped by a live-hunt capstone. Hunt in KQL, respond in minutes. Confirm current exam details on Microsoft Learn before you book — exam specifics change.",
      "objectives": [
        "Manage the SecOps environment: Sentinel workspaces, connectors, analytics rules, playbooks.",
        "Respond to incidents across Defender XDR, Endpoint, M365, Purview.",
        "Hunt in KQL: hypotheses first, queries second, notebooks for repeatability.",
        "Execute the live-hunt capstone: detect → investigate → contain → report."
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **Defender XDR** — the unified portal: incidents queue plus advanced hunting across the Microsoft security stack.\n- **Sentinel workspace** — the data boundary: connectors ingest here, rules query here, costs accrue here.\n- **data connector** — the ingestion on-ramp: Defender, Entra, firewalls, CEF/Syslog; connector first, rule second — detections need data.\n- **ingestion planning** — cost follows volume; high-volume noise gets filtered or sampled at the source, never blindly ingested.\n- **scheduled analytics rule** — KQL run on a cadence; the workhorse detection.\n- **NRT rule** — near-real-time analytics for the urgent detections that can’t wait for the schedule.\n- **automation playbook** — Logic Apps workflows: enrich the incident, then act — isolate the device, revoke the session; SOAR without the buzzword.\n- **MITRE ATT&CK mapping** — every rule mapped to technique; coverage gaps become the hunting and detection backlog.\n- **incident queue triage** — true positive? severity? blast radius? — the L1/L2 motion inside Sentinel.\n- **Defender for Endpoint** — device timelines and live response; contain the endpoint from the portal without touching the desk.\n- **M365 and Purview** — the email, Teams, SharePoint, DLP, and insider-risk surfaces an incident crosses.\n- **Microsoft Graph enrichment** — querying identity, device, and risk state programmatically; the API behind the portal.\n- **Security Copilot** — the assistant that drafts summaries and queries; the analyst verifies — the AI discipline, again.\n- **KQL pipe** — table | where | summarize: the hunting query shape; filter, aggregate, rank.\n- **summarize by bin()** — time-bucketing (bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)) for spotting beaconing and bursts on a timeline.\n- **KQL join** — correlating two tables (or a table with itself, as in impossible-travel hunts) on a shared key.\n- **hypothesis-first hunting** — ‘if the attacker used X, we’d see Y in table Z’ — then the query; never the query first.\n- **Sentinel notebooks** — Jupyter inside Sentinel: repeatable hunts and shared tradecraft the team can re-run.\n- **live-hunt capstone** — the multi-stage attack scenario: detect, investigate, contain, report; synthesis only, no new tools.\n- **SC-200 skills outline** — the exam’s published domains; confirm current details on Microsoft Learn before booking."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "You learn Sentinel by hunting in it. Every step below runs against lab or sample data — the motions are the skill, and the motions transfer to any workspace.\n1. **Stand up the environment on paper first.** Sketch your workspace boundary, list the data connectors you’d enable (Defender, Entra, firewall/CEF-Syslog), and mark what you’d filter at the source. Connector first, rule second — and ingestion is metered, so the sketch includes a cost note.\n2. **Write your first scheduled rule.** A KQL detection on a schedule: filter with where, aggregate with summarize, rank the results. Read the KQL before you’d trust any answer about it — the exam tests query comprehension exactly this way.\n3. **Add the NRT twin.** Decide which of your detections is urgent enough for near-real-time, and justify the choice in one sentence. Not everything deserves NRT.\n4. **Build the playbook.** A Logic Apps flow: enrich the incident (Graph: identity, device, risk state), then act (isolate device, revoke session) — then notify. Automation amplifies signal and noise alike, so the playbook only fires on a tuned rule.\n5. **Triage the queue.** Take a set of lab incidents: true positive? severity? blast radius? Practice the ransomware-on-three-hosts versus phishing-click-with-no-payload prioritization until it’s reflex.\n6. **Run a hypothesis-first hunt.** State the hypothesis (‘if the attacker used X, we’d see Y in table Z’), write the KQL — joins for correlation, bin() for timelines — and save it as a notebook so the team can re-run it.\n7. **Map to ATT&CK.** Tag every rule you wrote to its technique; the uncovered techniques are your hunting backlog. Threat-informed defense, in the workspace.\n8. **Execute the live-hunt capstone.** A multi-stage attack scenario, end to end: detect, investigate, contain, report. No new tools — synthesis only. The incident report is the deliverable."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "**Capstone: the live-hunt operation.** You are the on-shift analyst when the multi-stage scenario begins: a phishing wave into M365, a clicked link with no immediate payload, then lateral movement toward the lab domain controller. Detect each stage with analytics rules you wrote, investigate across Defender XDR timelines and Purview surfaces, enrich with Graph, contain via playbook actions (verify before the DC isolation — the AI discipline applies to your own runbooks too), and deliver the incident report: timeline, blast radius, root cause, ATT&CK mapping, and remediation with owners. Then write the exam-readiness note: which SC-200 domain each capstone step exercised. Confirm current exam details on Microsoft Learn — the outline moves."
        },
        {
          "title": "Noah check-in",
          "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- In one sentence, what is the deliverable I’m producing by the end of this module?\n- *Query drill:* “Noah gives you a hunting goal — top processes spawning cmd.exe. Talk Noah through the KQL shape before you write it: which table, which filter, which aggregation.”\n- Your playbook fires on every benign admin logon. Walk Noah through the tuning fix from the Hands-on procedure — rule-side, not playbook-side — and explain why.\n- *Honesty check:* Copilot suggests isolating the domain controller mid-incident. What do you verify first, and what happens if you click without verifying?"
        }
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "front": "Sentinel workspace = ?",
          "back": "The data boundary — connectors ingest, rules query, costs accrue here."
        },
        {
          "front": "Analytics rule types?",
          "back": "Scheduled (KQL batch), NRT (near-real-time), plus playbook automation."
        },
        {
          "front": "SC-200 heaviest area?",
          "back": "Environment + ATT&CK mapping, 40–45% — connectors, rules, playbooks."
        },
        {
          "front": "KQL hunt structure?",
          "back": "Hypothesis first: 'if X, we'd see Y in Z' — then the query, then the notebook."
        },
        {
          "front": "Live Response?",
          "back": "Run commands on endpoints from the Defender portal — contain remotely."
        },
        {
          "front": "Ingestion cost rule?",
          "back": "Filter at the source — metered ingestion punishes noise."
        },
        {
          "front": "KQL: count events per hour?",
          "back": "| summarize count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 1h) — bin() buckets the timestamps, summarize aggregates. The hunt's bread and butter."
        },
        {
          "front": "Scheduled vs NRT analytics rule?",
          "back": "Scheduled: batch query on a cadence (minutes to hours). NRT: ~1-minute latency for fast-moving threats — higher ingestion cost."
        }
      ],
